%[text] ## Fit Linearizable Nonlinear Data with Linear Regression Model
%[text] %[text:anchor:TMP_4107] In the next set of exercises, we will linearize the different types of functions and find the regression model of the linearized fit. Recall from [Curvilinear Regression](file:./CurvilinearRegression.m), we used the following transformations to linearize various functions. We also learned that exponential fits appear linear on **semilogy** plots while power data appear linear on **log-log** plots in the [Linearizable Nonlinear Regression](file:./CurvilinearRegression.m:M_29ff) section. 
%[text:table] %[text:anchor:M_5687]
%[text] | **Functions** | **Format** | **Linearized Format** |
%[text] | --- | --- | --- |
%[text] | Exponential | $y=ae^{bx}${"altText":"y=ae^{bx}"}  | ${\\ln(y)}=bx+{\\ln(a)}${"altText":"{\ln(y)}=bx+{\ln(a)}"} |
%[text] | Power | $y=ax^{b}${"altText":"y=ax^{b}"} | ${\\log(y)}=b\\thinspace{\\log(x)}+{\\log(a)}${"altText":"{\log(y)}=b\thinspace{\log(x)}+{\log(a)}"} |
%[text] | Saturation | $y=\\frac{x}{ax+b}${"altText":"y=\frac{x}{ax+b}"} | ${\\frac{1}y}=b\\frac{1}x+{a}${"altText":"{\frac{1}y}=b\frac{1}x+{a}"} |
%[text] | S-curve | $y=\\frac{1}{1+e^{-bx}${"altText":"y=\frac{1}{1+e^{-bx}"} | $\\ln\\left(\\frac{y}{1-y}\\right) =bx${"altText":"\ln\left(\frac{y}{1-y}\right) =bx"} |
%[text:table]
%[text] ### Generate Data
%[text] ![](text:image:5b3e) **Try**. Click **Generate Data**.
  %[control:button:07ed]{"position":[1,2]}
TP = GenPowData; % Use function to generate data
xPl = TP.xPl; % Extract the input variable from the table
yPl = TP.yPl; % Extract the response variable from the table
head(TP) % View top rows of table data and the column labels
%%
%[text] ### Plot Data
%[text] ![](text:image:4648) **Try**. Select an axis from the **Select an axis** dropdown menu below, and then click **Plot Data** to view the data plotted on different axes.
AX = "Select"; % Use this dropdown menu to select an axis on which to plot the data %[control:dropdown:8711]{"position":[6,14]}
plotDataByAxis(AX,xPl,yPl)
  % Click this button to plot the data %[control:button:2705]{"position":[1,2]}
%%
%[text] ### Determine the Appropriate Linearization Strategy
%[text] ![](text:image:8e98) **Exercise 1.** Based on the way the data fits on the various axes, standard x-y, semilogy, and log-log, which type of function best describes the data? Select and answer from the **Select an answer** dropdown menu and then click **Check Answer**.
Answer = "Select"; % Select an answer from this dropdown menu %[control:dropdown:28ad]{"position":[10,18]}
checkAnswer(1,Answer)
  % Click this button to check your answer %[control:button:2545]{"position":[1,2]}
%%
%[text] ### Linearize Data
%[text] ![](text:image:0cbb) **Try**. We linearize the data using the appropriate[ linearization strategy](internal:M_5687) for the data below. Click **Linearize Data**, to linearize data.
% Ensure log(x) and log(y) are real numbers and remove non-positive values
valid = xPl > 0 & yPl > 0 & isfinite(xPl) & isfinite(yPl);

% Additionally remove any negative or zero entries explicitly (defensive)
valid = valid & (xPl > 0) & (yPl > 0);

xP1 = xPl(valid);
yP1 = yPl(valid);
plotDataByAxis("log-log",xP1,yP1)
  %[control:button:7212]{"position":[1,2]}
%%
%[text] ### Perform Linear Regression of Linearized Model
%[text] In [Practice Linear](file:./TryRegression.m), we explored how to calculate regression line coefficients in MATLAB and plot them alongside a scatter plot. The great part about linearizing an equation is that it turns a traditionally difficult, nonlinear problem into something we can handle with the same simple linear regression techniques we already know. It’s like unlocking a shortcut to analyze complex data!
%[text] ![](text:image:4d4b) **Try**. We can use the [`fitlm`](https://www.mathworks.com/help/stats/fitlm.html) function in MATLAB to calculate the coefficients for a regression line, and display the scatter plot with the regression line and the 95% confidence interval. Click **Perform Linear Regression** to perform linear regression on the linearized model.
logx = log10(xP1);
logy = log10(yP1);
mdl = fitlm(logx,logy); % Fit linear model to linearized data
plot(mdl) % Plot the fitted regression line with diagnostics
xlabel("log(x)")
ylabel("log(y)")
title("Linearized Power Fit")
legend("show",Location="best")
hold on % Retain current plot on axes
  %[control:button:66fc]{"position":[1,2]}
%%
%[text] ### Use Linear Regression Model to Predict One Response
%[text] ![](text:image:65fd) **Try**. We use the [`predict`](https://www.mathworks.com/help/stats/linearmodel.predict.html?s_tid=srchtitle_support_results_3_predict#d126e1014929) function in MATLAB to predict a response for $\\log(x) = 0.67${"altText":"\log(x) = 0.67"} with the linearized regression model
% Predict the responses of the linear regression model
logyPred = predict(mdl,0.67); % Use the fitted model to predict y values for the input x Try.
%[text] ![](text:image:80bf) **Try**. In addition, we overlay a [`scatter`](https://www.mathworks.com/help/matlab/ref/scatter.html?s_tid=srchtitle_support_results_1_scatter) plot of the predicted values on the response plot above, using filled diamond markers with a marker size of 60 points. Click **Plot Predicted Value** to plot the predicted value for $\\log(x) = 0.67${"altText":"\log(x) = 0.67"}.
% Plot original data and predictions
scatter(0.67,logyPred,60,"filled","d",DisplayName="Prediction for 0.67",SeriesIndex="none")
legend("show",Location="best") % Add a legend to the plot
title("Linearized Power Data + Prediction")
hold off % Release all plots from the axes
  %[control:button:73e1]{"position":[1,2]}
%%
%[text] ![](text:image:4015) **Exercise 2.** Recall that with our linearized data, we applied a log10 transform to both x and y. So for $\\log(x)=0.67${"altText":"\log(x)=0.67"}, we know that $x=10^{0.67}= 4.68${"altText":"x=10^{0.67}= 4.68"}. Calculate the value for$yPred${"altText":"yPred"} below.
% Enter your code here.
